artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the setting of statistical and functional genetics in humans, where a quantitative or disease trait T is influenced by many genetic variants distributed across the genome. Suppose that for a given trait T, a particular variant v (a single-nucleotide polymorphism, indel, or structural variant) or a gene g has been causally linked to T, in the sense that interventions on v or g would change the distribution of T at the population level. Assume further that:

(i) most such causal variants v reside in noncoding regions of the genome (i.e., outside protein-coding exons), and individually exert only weak effects on T;
(ii) the trait T is highly polygenic, or even omnigenic, in the sense that a very large number of variants—potentially across most expressed genes—contribute small, often comparable effects to T;
(iii) controlled, randomized, organism-level perturbation experiments in living humans are not feasible, so any mechanistic inference must rely on observational human data, model systems (cell lines, organoids, model organisms), and indirect or in vitro perturbation assays.

Devise general analytical and/or experimental strategies that, given such a causal variant v or causal gene g for trait T, identify or substantially narrow down the biological mechanism—i.e., the chain of molecular, cellular, tissue-level, and physiological processes—through which v or g influences T. A successful strategy should:

(a) be applicable to weak-effect noncoding variants whose direct molecular consequence is not immediately apparent;
(b) account for the polygenic/omnigenic architecture of T, including the possibility that the relevant mechanism operates through diffuse regulatory networks rather than a single core pathway;
(c) make precise, testable predictions about intermediate molecular phenotypes (e.g., chromatin state, transcript abundance, protein activity, cell-state composition) that can be validated using human cell or tissue assays, model organisms, or population-scale multi-omic data;
(d) come with a principled framework for evaluating when a proposed mechanism is established, including criteria distinguishing genuine mechanistic links from correlative associations.

Provide such a strategy, or prove rigorous obstructions showing that, under stated assumptions about the available data and assays, no strategy in a specified class can recover the mechanism.

